1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,734
Every agent you built in Sections 3 to 6 worked the moment you tested it against

2
00:00:04,734 --> 00:00:05,391
pinned data.

3
00:00:05,391 --> 00:00:09,830
That was always going to be true, because you built the test data to be readable.

4
00:00:09,830 --> 00:00:14,323
A real trial has to include the cases you did not think to plan for, because those

5
00:00:14,323 --> 00:00:16,899
are exactly the ones a real week will hand you.

6
00:00:16,899 --> 00:00:20,735
Why is "it worked three times" so persuasive when it proves so little?

7
00:00:20,735 --> 00:00:23,036
Because agreement feels like accumulation.

8
00:00:23,036 --> 00:00:27,475
Three right answers in a row feel like three pieces of evidence stacking up, when

9
00:00:27,475 --> 00:00:32,023
what they usually mean is that Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday happened to be easy in

10
00:00:32,023 --> 00:00:32,735
the same way.

11
00:00:32,735 --> 00:00:36,955
Priya's first week of triage flags came back clean, every one, and the honest

12
00:00:36,955 --> 00:00:40,023
reading of that week was not "the agent is trustworthy".

13
00:00:40,023 --> 00:00:42,325
It was "the inbox was ordinary this week".

14
00:00:42,325 --> 00:00:46,818
The whole discipline of this section lives in the gap between those two sentences,

15
00:00:46,818 --> 00:00:50,928
and the trial is the tool that closes it: not more days of agreement, but a

16
00:00:50,928 --> 00:00:53,394
deliberate search for the day that disagrees.

17
00:00:53,944 --> 00:00:56,000
Go back to Section 3.5 for a second.

18
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,682
The pinned inbox was not forty one ordinary emails, it was forty ordinary ones and

19
00:01:00,682 --> 00:01:02,909
one that mattered, deliberately buried.

20
00:01:02,909 --> 00:01:07,705
That is what a testing mindset looks like, and it is the same mindset a proper trial

21
00:01:07,705 --> 00:01:10,845
needs, just aimed at real weeks instead of pinned data.

22
00:01:10,845 --> 00:01:15,527
Each of your four agents has a case designed to break it, and you already know all

23
00:01:15,527 --> 00:01:20,152
four, because each build named its own; the next slide gathers them in one place.

24
00:01:20,152 --> 00:01:24,892
A trial that happens to contain none of them has tested the weather, not the agent,

25
00:01:24,892 --> 00:01:29,631
and the practical move follows directly: when the breaking case does not show up on

26
00:01:29,631 --> 00:01:30,773
its own, feed it in.

27
00:01:30,773 --> 00:01:32,771
Send yourself the borderline email.

28
00:01:32,771 --> 00:01:35,341
Walk back a commitment in your own test call.

29
00:01:35,341 --> 00:01:37,510
You are allowed to make the exam hard.

30
00:01:37,510 --> 00:01:39,052
That is what exams are for.

31
00:01:39,602 --> 00:01:43,936
Keep this table beside your trial log, because it is the difference between waiting

32
00:01:43,936 --> 00:01:45,764
for a hard week and scheduling one.

33
00:01:45,764 --> 00:01:50,046
Each row is an exam question you can set deliberately, and a trial that has passed

34
00:01:50,046 --> 00:01:54,328
its own breaking case has earned something a lucky fortnight never earns: you have

35
00:01:54,328 --> 00:01:58,141
watched this agent be wrong, or nearly wrong, and watched what caught it.

36
00:01:58,691 --> 00:02:01,597
That last point catches more people than any other.

37
00:02:01,597 --> 00:02:06,271
A trial with no defined end quietly becomes "I'll always check it a bit," which is

38
00:02:06,271 --> 00:02:09,463
not the same as either Trial or Review from Section 1.2.

39
00:02:09,463 --> 00:02:12,711
Decide the length up front, in writing, before you start.

40
00:02:12,711 --> 00:02:17,328
Notice that you have already done this four times if you followed the builds: one

41
00:02:17,328 --> 00:02:21,945
week for triage, three or four calls for notes, three Mondays for the report, two

42
00:02:21,945 --> 00:02:26,732
weeks of drafts for the chaser, each written into a job description before its agent

43
00:02:26,732 --> 00:02:26,960
ran.

44
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:29,867
The third rule is where the ceilings come back too.

45
00:02:29,867 --> 00:02:34,540
Each trial watches for one specific betrayal: a query marked resolved, an invented

46
00:02:34,540 --> 00:02:39,100
date, a closed status the sheet never granted, a draft that reads like pressure.

47
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:40,924
You are not vaguely supervising.

48
00:02:40,924 --> 00:02:45,426
You are standing guard at a named door, and knowing which door changes what you

49
00:02:45,426 --> 00:02:45,825
notice.

50
00:02:46,375 --> 00:02:51,022
Hold onto the asymmetry underneath all of this, because it is the reason the section

51
00:02:51,022 --> 00:02:55,668
exists, and it is the last thing this lecture asks you to believe on argument alone:

52
00:02:55,668 --> 00:02:58,267
the next lecture's incident shows it happening.

53
00:02:58,267 --> 00:03:02,748
Confidence without checking feels identical to confidence with checking, right up

54
00:03:02,748 --> 00:03:07,062
until the day it does not, and by then the unchecked agent has weeks of output

55
00:03:07,062 --> 00:03:07,615
behind it.

56
00:03:07,615 --> 00:03:12,151
The checklist in the next lecture is how you make the difference between those two

57
00:03:12,151 --> 00:03:13,755
confidences visible on paper.

58
00:03:13,755 --> 00:03:14,529
See you there.
